        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <p><strong>OBJECTIVES:</strong></p>
<p>When a person stands after an extended period of inactivity, there is a pooling of blood in the lower part of the body and legs. This leads to a reduced filling of the heart resulting in a decrease in blood pressure and syncope (brief loss of consciousness), if reflex regulation of the circulation is impeded. An important mechanism which is activated to protect the circulation is a local reflex in muscle and skin (local veno-arteriolar reflex). This reflex is elicited by the pooling of blood in the legs during orthostatic intolerance. Activation of these local reflexes results in constriction of the small blood vessels in skin and muscle tissue which increase the resistance to blood flow and assists in maintenance of blood pressure during upright posture. After space flight the ability to regulate blood pressure while upright is attenuated, which can severely inhibit the functional cerebral capacity of crewmembers during re-entry and landing. At present there is no effective treatment to prevent spaceflight-induced orthostatic intolerance. The effect of weightlessness on the function of this subcutaneous local vasoconstrictor reflex was investigated by the use of local <sup>133</sup>Xenon washout technique that monitors local blood flow. This experiment was performed to investigate whether the local veno-arteriolar reflex is active, attenuated, or absent following exposure to weightlessness.</p>
<p><strong>APPROACH:</strong></p>
<p>During this study, the local veno-arteriolar reflex are activated by lowering the lower leg relative to the heart. Subcutaneous blood flow in the lower leg (<sup>133</sup>Xenon washout) and arterial blood pressure were measured in the relaxed supine position and during lowering of the leg relative to the heart. The local vascular resistance was calculated by mean arterial pressure divided by the local blood flow.</p>
<p>There were no inflight operations for this experiment. Before and after flight, a small amount of a radioactive isotope, <sup>133</sup>Xenon, dissolved in sterile saline solution was injected into the subcutaneous tissue of the outer lower leg (approximately 10 cm above the ankle) of participating crewmembers. The crewmember was instrumented with an Arterial Blood Pressure Measurement Device, and arterial blood pressure was recorded continuously throughout the experiment. This measurement was used to calculate subcutaneous vascular resistance, which helps to regulate arterial blood pressure and prevent orthostatic hypotension (dizziness when standing after an extended period of inactivity). The Xenon Detector Unit was circled slowly over the injection area, while the Xenon Memory Box displayed the counting rate. The position of maximal counting rate was marked with a pen, and the Xenon Detector Unit was secured in place over the marked position. After 30 minutes had elapsed from the time of injection, the subcutaneous blood flow in the lower leg was measured (<sup>133</sup>Xenon washout technique) while the crewmember was in a relaxed supine position (for 7 minutes), during leg lowering 40 cm relative to the heart (7 minutes), and again during the relaxed supine position (for 7 minutes). Following completion of the protocol, the Xenon Memory Box was connected to a computer using a RS-232 interface, and the counting rates were downloaded. This protocol was performed once preflight and once immediately postflight</p>
<p><strong>RESULTS:</strong></p>
<p>The findings in this study are similar to some recently reported observations. During a week in space, astronauts exhibit an increase in cardiac output of 22% without an increase in mean arterial pressure compared with when upright seated on the ground. Thus, systemic vascular resistance is decreased in space.</p>
<p>The subcutaneous venoarteriolar reflex in the lower legs is not attenuated by prolonged weightlessness in space for up to 195 days. Thus, the venoarteriolar reflex does not need to be regularly exercised to maintain efficiency. Therefore, edema formation and capillary damage are unlikely to be obstacles in astronauts on landing even after very long missions.</p>
